WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:05.360
What was the first company in the world to
reach a valuation of $2 trillion?

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:09.290
The most common guess would probably be Apple
or Microsoft.

00:00:09.290 --> 00:00:14.930
But the first company to reach a $2 trillion
valuation isn’t a tech company nor is it

00:00:14.930 --> 00:00:15.930
American.

00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:20.030
Saudi Aramco which is Saudi Arabia’s oil
company was actually the first company to

00:00:20.030 --> 00:00:24.640
reach a $2 trillion valuation in December
of 2019.

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:29.320
And given that Saudi Aramco was making more
money than Apple and Google combined before

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:33.700
the pandemic, it’s no wonder why they’re
worth so much.

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:38.120
What is surprising though is the fact that
Saudi Arabia didn’t actually found Aramco,

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:39.840
they actually bought it.

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:46.530
So, here’s how Saudi Arabia acquired one
of the most valuable companies in the world.

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:51.470
It all started in the early 1900s when Henry
Ford introduced the Model T, the world’s

00:00:51.470 --> 00:00:52.470
first mass-produced vehicle.

00:00:52.470 --> 00:00:57.890
The Model T was quite popular from the very
beginning, but as more competition entered

00:00:57.890 --> 00:01:02.622
the scene, automobiles went from being a luxury
to being a valuable consumer good in the 1920s

00:01:02.622 --> 00:01:04.650
and 30s.

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:09.130
This sudden increase in automobile ownership
created a strong demand for cheap oil, so

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:13.520
companies like Standard Oil and Shell started
scouring the earth for the richest oil reserves

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:14.880
they could find.

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:19.590
In the meantime, modern Saudi Arabia was just
starting to take shape.

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:25.859
The Abdulaziz family had ruled central Arabia
for 130 years, but in 1891, a rivaling group

00:01:25.859 --> 00:01:29.340
would conquer the region and push out the
former ruling family.

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:35.030
For over a decade, the Abdulaziz family stood
back and relinquished their control until

00:01:35.030 --> 00:01:38.869
one of their descendants Ibn Saud decided
to take back control.

00:01:38.869 --> 00:01:44.590
In 1902, Ibn Saud led a revolt with 40 people
into Riyadh, and the group would successfully

00:01:44.590 --> 00:01:48.029
take back Riyadh making Ibn the ruler of the
area.

00:01:48.029 --> 00:01:53.749
Ibn spent the next 30 years conquering most
of Arabia, and it wasn’t until 1932 that

00:01:53.749 --> 00:01:57.990
he would unite the regions of Arabia and create
modern-day Saudi Arabia.

00:01:57.990 --> 00:02:02.329
Having successfully conquered the entire region,
king Ibn turned his focus onto advancing the

00:02:02.329 --> 00:02:04.029
kingdom’s economic state.

00:02:04.029 --> 00:02:08.459
Just a couple of months before Saudi Arabia
was formed, the SoCal oil company, or now

00:02:08.459 --> 00:02:12.409
Chevron, struck oil in Bahrain in May of 1932.

00:02:12.409 --> 00:02:16.450
This discovery piqued interest in the middle
eastern oil search, and King Ibn would quickly

00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:19.280
leverage this opportunity the following year.

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:24.890
In November of 1933, King Ibn signed a concession
agreement with the SoCal.

00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:28.690
This agreement created a subsidiary company
called the California Arabia Standard Oil

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:32.840
Company or CASOC which was fully owned by
SoCal.

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:37.319
Casoc immediately began work in Saudi Arabia
starting by surveying the area over the next

00:02:37.319 --> 00:02:38.340
year.

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:43.939
Casoc started drilling in 1935, but despite
a year of planning, Casoc didn’t find any

00:02:43.939 --> 00:02:44.939
oil.

00:02:44.939 --> 00:02:49.620
Discouraged by the lack of progress, SoCal
would sell 50% of the company to Texaco, and

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:52.120
this kept the effort alive for a few more
years.

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:58.290
By 1937 though, Casoc was still yet to find
any oil, so SoCal considered giving up on

00:02:58.290 --> 00:03:01.819
the endeavor altogether.

00:03:01.819 --> 00:03:05.849
SoCal consulted with their chief geologist
Max Steineke who recommended that they should

00:03:05.849 --> 00:03:06.909
keep drilling.

00:03:06.909 --> 00:03:12.640
So, the executives kept the operation alive,
but they weren’t particularly optimistic.

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:17.859
Just a year later though, Casoc struck commercial
quantities of oil at Dammam Well No. 7.

00:03:17.859 --> 00:03:20.790
This well would later be named the prosperity
well.

00:03:20.790 --> 00:03:26.670
Anyway, Saudi Arabia would export their first
tanker of oil in 1939, and with that Saudi

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:29.129
Arabia had entered the oil industry.

00:03:29.129 --> 00:03:34.360
Saudi Arabia was only making a small royalty
on all of the oil being exported, but at this

00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:38.389
point, King Ibn was simply happy to have this
opportunity, so he wouldn’t make a fuss

00:03:38.389 --> 00:03:39.389
about it.

00:03:39.389 --> 00:03:45.150
A few years later, in 1941, Casoc discovered
oil in Abqaiq, and Casoc would rapidly expand

00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:46.780
across Saudi Arabia throughout the 1940s.

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:52.939
In 1944, the company’s name was changed
from California Arabian Standard Oil to Arabian

00:03:52.939 --> 00:03:55.279
American Oil or Aramco.

00:03:55.279 --> 00:04:00.019
Soon enough, more American oil companies wanted
a part of the pie, and they would end up investing

00:04:00.019 --> 00:04:02.420
quite heavily in 1948.

00:04:02.420 --> 00:04:07.519
Standard Oil of New Jersey which would later
become Exxon purchased a 30% stake in Aramco.

00:04:07.519 --> 00:04:12.279
And Socony Vacuum which would later become
Mobil purchased a 10% stake in the company.

00:04:12.279 --> 00:04:16.630
In the meantime, though Saudia Arabia wasn’t
making too much money from the operation every

00:04:16.630 --> 00:04:21.380
year, the operation had been going on for
a decade and Saudi Arabia had saved up a good

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:23.180
amount of money over the years.

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:27.110
And given all of this interest in Aramco,
King Ibn realized that he finally had the

00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:28.300
upper hand.

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:33.360
In 1949, Aramco would accidentally drill into
the emirate of Abu Dhabi.

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:38.009
This created a border dispute between Saudi
Arabia and Abu Dhabi and gave King Ibn the

00:04:38.009 --> 00:04:40.280
perfect opportunity to squeeze Aramco.

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:44.680
King Ibn threatened that since Aramco couldn’t
respect the countries’ borders, Saudi Arabia

00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:47.990
was going to nationalize their oil production
and kick out the Americans.

00:04:47.990 --> 00:04:53.150
In response, the Americans would bend over
and agree to share 50% of Aramco’s profits

00:04:53.150 --> 00:04:54.740
with Saudi Arabia.

00:04:54.740 --> 00:04:58.800
Aramco also agreed to move its headquarters
from New York to the Saudi Arabian city of

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:00.280
Dhahran.

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:05.080
Saudi Arabia still had no stake in the company,
but 50% profit sharing was way better than

00:05:05.080 --> 00:05:07.710
the small royalty they were previously receiving.

00:05:07.710 --> 00:05:12.389
Moving into the 1950s, Aramco transitioned
from being a large oil producer to being an

00:05:12.389 --> 00:05:13.750
oil giant.

00:05:13.750 --> 00:05:19.139
In 1950, they completed the 1200 kilometer
Trans-Arabia Pipeline which connected eastern

00:05:19.139 --> 00:05:21.669
Saudi Arabia with the Mediterranean sea.

00:05:21.669 --> 00:05:26.420
They also discovered the Safaniyah oil field
in 1951 which became the world’s largest

00:05:26.420 --> 00:05:28.590
offshore oil field at the time.

00:05:28.590 --> 00:05:33.550
By 1958, Aramco was producing over 1 million
barrels of oil per day.

00:05:33.550 --> 00:05:38.130
And while that’s quite a milestone, that’s
not even 10% of how much they produce today.

00:05:38.130 --> 00:05:43.800
So, there was still a lot of growth left,
and the next pivotal point in Saudi Arabia’s

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:46.599
oil journey was the foundation of OPEC.

00:05:46.599 --> 00:05:52.200
In 1960, 5 of the top oil-producing countries
came together in Baghdad to form a sort of

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:53.200
collaboration.

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:56.139
While these countries produced a significant
proportion of the world’s oil, they were

00:05:56.139 --> 00:06:00.259
still very much dependent on America and the
Soviet Union.

00:06:00.259 --> 00:06:04.229
They figured that they could reduce this dependency
by coming together and creating a sort of

00:06:04.229 --> 00:06:06.690
monopoly on Middle Eastern oil.

00:06:06.690 --> 00:06:11.860
The first time OPEC leveraged this power was
in 1973 and this would become known as the

00:06:11.860 --> 00:06:14.550
oil crisis of 1973.

00:06:14.550 --> 00:06:18.919
At the time, the Yom Kippur War was taking
place between Israel and a coalition of Arab

00:06:18.919 --> 00:06:21.169
states led by Egypt and Syria.

00:06:21.169 --> 00:06:25.930
The US and several western countries were
passively supporting Israel during the war,

00:06:25.930 --> 00:06:29.169
and Saudi Arabia wasn’t a big fan of this.

00:06:29.169 --> 00:06:33.010
In retaliation, Saudia Arabia would get OPEC
to enforce an oil embargo on all of these

00:06:33.010 --> 00:06:34.539
western countries.

00:06:34.539 --> 00:06:39.330
To make things even worse, the US was already
facing an oil shortage due to strong economic

00:06:39.330 --> 00:06:43.139
growth in the 1960s which skyrocketed automobile
ownership.

00:06:43.139 --> 00:06:48.860
So, when OPEC announced the embargo, global
oil prices surged from $3 per barrel to $12

00:06:48.860 --> 00:06:49.889
per barrel.

00:06:49.889 --> 00:06:53.800
This caused massive panic within western countries
as people raced to gas stations to fill up

00:06:53.800 --> 00:06:55.159
their vehicles.

00:06:55.159 --> 00:06:59.300
Gas stations across the country quickly dried
up and gas stations started to impose limits

00:06:59.300 --> 00:07:02.180
on how much gas an individual could buy.

00:07:02.180 --> 00:07:07.259
President Nixon even limited all highways
to a speed limit of 55 mph as this was thought

00:07:07.259 --> 00:07:09.610
to be the most fuel-efficient speed.

00:07:09.610 --> 00:07:13.580
Despite the internal crisis that the oil embargo
caused, the US didn’t actually change its

00:07:13.580 --> 00:07:18.790
stance on Israel and Israeli forces didn’t
retreat to the 1949 Armistice line.

00:07:18.790 --> 00:07:23.430
So, the oil embargo was technically a failure.

00:07:23.430 --> 00:07:28.319
But what this did do was aggravate Saudi Arabia.

00:07:28.319 --> 00:07:32.919
Saudi Arabia had worked with the Americans
for 41 years, and they had shared 50% of the

00:07:32.919 --> 00:07:34.470
profit for 24 years.

00:07:34.470 --> 00:07:39.069
So, they had plenty of money by this point,
and they decided that it was time to kick

00:07:39.069 --> 00:07:40.560
out the Americans.

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:45.280
In 1973, the Saudi Arabian government bought
a 25% stake in the company.

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:48.500
In 1974, they would increase this stake to
60%.

00:07:48.500 --> 00:07:54.229
And, in 1980, the Saudi government would increase
their stake to 100%, taking full control of

00:07:54.229 --> 00:07:55.229
the company.

00:07:55.229 --> 00:08:00.060
Al Naimi would become the company’s first
Saudi president in 1984 and eventually CEO

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:01.699
in 1988.

00:08:01.699 --> 00:08:07.099
The name of the company would also be changed
from just Aramco to Saudi Aramco in 1988.

00:08:07.099 --> 00:08:12.030
And with that, Saudi Aramco began its transition
from being a leading oil exporter to being

00:08:12.030 --> 00:08:14.449
a global petroleum enterprise.

00:08:14.449 --> 00:08:19.409
In 1991, Saudi Aramco purchased a 35% stake
in Ssangyong Oil Refining Company located

00:08:19.409 --> 00:08:20.789
in Korea.

00:08:20.789 --> 00:08:26.069
In 1994, they purchased a 40% stake in Petron
Corporation located in the Philippines.

00:08:26.069 --> 00:08:31.259
In 1996, they acquired a 50% stake in Corinth
Refineries located in Europe.

00:08:31.259 --> 00:08:35.650
Around the same time, Saudi Aramco also played
with simulation software.

00:08:35.650 --> 00:08:40.350
This probably sounds like one of the most
boring things in the world, but Saudi Aramco

00:08:40.350 --> 00:08:44.460
developed an oil reservoir simulator called
POWERS in 1997.

00:08:44.460 --> 00:08:48.280
Though that sounds super monotonous, POWERS
helped them significantly reduce storage and

00:08:48.280 --> 00:08:49.890
transportation costs.

00:08:49.890 --> 00:08:55.200
They followed up POWERS with GigaPOWERS in
2010 and eventually TeraPOWERS in 2016.

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:59.190
All of these acquisitions and technological
developments have allowed Saudi Aramco to

00:08:59.190 --> 00:09:04.320
become the biggest oil company in the world
by far; however, Saudi Arabia still had a

00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:09.580
massive problem on their hands which was that
their country was basically entirely dependent

00:09:09.580 --> 00:09:10.800
on oil.

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:16.920
And this was made painfully evident in 2014
when oil prices plummeted over 50%.

00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:21.990
Saudi Arabia quickly fell into a massive government
deficit and their national debt has skyrocketed

00:09:21.990 --> 00:09:23.030
ever since.

00:09:23.030 --> 00:09:29.950
In 2014, Saudi Arabia had almost no debt with
a debt to GDP ratio of 1.6%.

00:09:29.950 --> 00:09:32.340
By 2020, that number was 32.5%.

00:09:32.340 --> 00:09:39.000
I gotta say though, that’s still nowhere
near America’s debt to GDP ratio of 140%.

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:43.940
Anyway, unlike the US, the Saudis decided
that they had to do something about their

00:09:43.940 --> 00:09:44.940
debt.

00:09:44.940 --> 00:09:50.020
So, the prince of Saudi Arabia, Mohammed Bin
Salman launched a plan called Vision 2030.

00:09:50.020 --> 00:09:54.400
The plan entails diversifying the country’s
dependence on oil by investing in tourism,

00:09:54.400 --> 00:09:56.470
infrastructure, and recreation.

00:09:56.470 --> 00:10:01.691
This is why Saudi Arabia spent $3.8 billion
on expanding their airport and why the country

00:10:01.691 --> 00:10:04.100
finally started giving out tourist visas.

00:10:04.100 --> 00:10:08.150
One of the ways Mohammed bin Salman funded
this diversification effort was by taking

00:10:08.150 --> 00:10:09.610
Saudi Aramco public.

00:10:09.610 --> 00:10:16.100
And with $111 billion in net profit, investors
didn’t have a hard time justifying a $2

00:10:16.100 --> 00:10:18.610
trillion valuation for Saudi Aramco.

00:10:18.610 --> 00:10:23.310
Saudi Arabia only sold a few percent of Saudi
Aramco, but that itself added up to tens of

00:10:23.310 --> 00:10:24.310
billions.

00:10:24.310 --> 00:10:28.930
So, at the end of the day, how did Saudi Arabia
buy a $2 trillion company?

00:10:28.930 --> 00:10:33.010
Well, the first step was being blessed with
the world’s second-largest oil reserve.

00:10:33.010 --> 00:10:36.830
The second step was partnering with the Americans
and living off of dividends and profit-sharing

00:10:36.830 --> 00:10:37.970
for 40 years.

00:10:37.970 --> 00:10:41.570
The third step was using saving up this money
and eventually buying out Aramco.

00:10:41.570 --> 00:10:47.330
The fourth step was continuing monumental
growth and making international investments.

00:10:47.330 --> 00:10:52.570
Finally, the last step was going public as
the most profitable company in the world.

00:10:52.570 --> 00:10:56.260
And that’s how Saudi Arabia bought the world’s
first $2 trillion company.

00:10:56.260 --> 00:11:00.070
Do you guys think Saudi Aramco is worth $2
trillion given the trend towards renewable

00:11:00.070 --> 00:11:01.070
energy?

00:11:01.070 --> 00:11:02.210
Comment that down below.

00:11:02.210 --> 00:11:06.940
Also, drop a like if you’re excited to move
away from our dependence on oil.

00:11:06.940 --> 00:11:14.950
And of course, consider subscribing to see
more questions logically answered.

